diff --git a/docs/bots.md b/docs/bots.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..91ca848
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bots.md
@@ -0,0 +1,261 @@
+# Chat bots
+
+Any signed-in member can open **Set up chat bots** from account settings to
+create bots. Members manage their own bots; administrators manage all bots.
+Names, optional avatars, optional webhook URLs, and **Add to all open rooms**
+are editable. Each bot's
+read-only **Bot code** is a bearer credential, not a public identifier.
+
+## Bots per member
+
+Administrators can set **Bots per member** in **Account settings** (`/account/edit`).
+It defaults to **5**, including on existing accounts. The setting accepts a
+non-negative whole number; **0** disables new bot creation for members.
+Administrators are always exempt from the cap.
+
+Each member's quota counts only the bots they own. Deleting a bot frees a slot;
+bots owned by other people do not use it. At or above the limit, the bot listing
+shows **reached the limit** instead of **Add a chat bot**. The new/create routes
+also refuse requests, including submissions from previously opened forms, and
+creation validation enforces the quota outside the controller.
+
+Lowering the limit never deletes bots or changes their memberships, codes, or
+webhooks. Existing bots remain listed and editable even above the new limit;
+only new creation is blocked until the member is below the limit again. Raising
+the limit takes effect immediately.
+
+The limit uses the existing typed account settings JSON (`bots_per_member`), so
+no database migration or bot backfill is required.
+
+## Posting
+
+Bots do **not** automatically join open rooms by default. People still do.
+Use the room editor's **Give BOT_NAME access to this room** checkbox to add a bot
+to individual open or closed rooms. A bot can only post to rooms it belongs to.
+
+Enable **Add to all open rooms** on the new or edit bot form to join every existing
+open room and every room created or converted to open later. It is off by default.
+Turning it off keeps all current memberships; it only stops future automatic
+joins. Turning it on again adds any missing open-room memberships. Closed and
+direct rooms are never automatically joined. Existing bots keep their memberships
+when upgrading, but default to not automatically joining future rooms.
+
+```sh
+curl -i --data-binary 'Deploy of v1.2.3 finished ✅' \
+  "https://chat.example.com/rooms/$ROOM_ID/$BOT_CODE/messages"
+```
+
+The POST body is UTF-8 plain text, with no parameter names or JSON envelope.
+HTML-looking text is displayed literally. This endpoint uses the bot code
+instead of browser sessions or CSRF tokens. It returns:
+
+- `201`, with `Location: /messages/:message_id` after creating and broadcasting.
+- `422` for empty/whitespace-only text outside cooldown (the raw-text endpoint has no attachment).
+- `404` for an unknown room or a room the bot has not joined.
+- `401` for an invalid code, including deleted, banned, or deactivated bots.
+- `429` with `Retry-After` during a posting cooldown.
+- `403` for a suspended bot.
+
+Messages use the existing unread tracking, broadcasts, and push notification
+job, with the bot user as their author.
+
+## Mention webhooks
+
+Set the optional **Webhook URL** when adding or editing a bot. Clearing the
+field removes the webhook; the bot's posting code and asynchronous posting
+endpoint remain available. URLs must be absolute HTTP or HTTPS URLs without
+embedded user/password credentials. Use HTTPS in production.
+
+Bots appear in the same `@` autocomplete as people. Select the bot's mention in
+the composer: simply typing its name as ordinary text does not create a mention.
+For each new human-authored message, each mentioned active bot with a webhook
+and room membership gets one HTTP POST, even if mentioned repeatedly.
+
+The request has `Content-Type: application/json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "mention",
+  "author": { "id": 7, "name": "Alice" },
+  "room": {
+    "id": 5,
+    "name": "Ops",
+    "reply_to": "/rooms/5/YOUR_BOT_CODE/messages"
+  },
+  "message": {
+    "id": 118,
+    "body": {
+      "text": "restart the build please",
+      "html": "<div class=\"trix-content\">… full rich-text body including the mention …</div>"
+    },
+    "link": "/rooms/5/@118"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+- `event` is `mention` here; consumers should also handle `joined`, below.
+- `body.text` removes this bot's mention attachments (not other mentions or
+  literal `@name` text) and trims surrounding Unicode whitespace, including
+  Trix's non-breaking mention separator. Internal spacing/line breaks remain.
+- `body.html` is the full rendered rich-text body, including the mention.
+- `reply_to` contains **this bot's** posting credential for this room. Keep the
+  payload private, and do not log this URL. POST a raw UTF-8 plain-text body to
+  this path to reply later, as described above.
+- `link` opens the room at the mentioned message. Both paths are relative to
+  the Campfire origin, which the bot must know.
+
+A webhook can reply immediately:
+
+- `200` and `Content-Type: text/plain`: the body is posted as literal text.
+- `200` and `Content-Type: text/html`: the body is posted as rich text, using
+  Action Text's normal rendering/sanitization.
+- `204`: no message is posted. The bot can still post asynchronously later.
+
+Empty bodies, other statuses, and unsupported media types produce no reply.
+Media-type parameters such as `charset=utf-8` are supported. Replies use the
+normal bot author, room broadcasts, unread tracking, and push notifications.
+
+## Joined webhooks
+
+Whenever a bot is first added to an open or closed room, whether automatically or
+by a person, its configured webhook receives:
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "joined",
+  "room": {
+    "id": 5,
+    "name": "Ops",
+    "reply_to": "/rooms/5/YOUR_BOT_CODE/messages"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+There is no `author` or `message` in a `joined` payload. The same `200` plain-text
+or HTML response rules apply, so the bot can greet the room immediately. The
+`reply_to` path also works for asynchronous posting.
+
+Each bot gets one `joined` event per room. Repeated grants, room renaming,
+switching automatic joining off and back on, and removal/re-addition do not send
+another event for that room. Direct conversations **never** trigger `joined`
+(mentions in direct conversations still work). Bots without a webhook at the
+time of joining do not receive retrospective events when a webhook is configured.
+Existing memberships are not notified retroactively during an upgrade.
+
+### Delivery and safety
+
+Mention callbacks run in `User::Bot::WebhookJob`, enqueued **after message
+creation commits**. Joined callbacks run in `User::Bot::JoinedWebhookJob`, enqueued
+**after membership creation commits**. There is no outbound HTTP or DNS lookup
+in the posting or room-management request.
+Production needs the existing Resque workers running. Webhook failures never
+undo the human's message or the membership grant. Jobs for deleted
+bots/messages/rooms are discarded; removal of the webhook, deactivation/banning, and room access revocation are checked at
+delivery. Access and configuration are checked again before posting a reply.
+
+Like Campfire's link fetcher, webhook delivery uses the private-network guard:
+only public destinations are contacted, the resolved address is pinned for the
+connection, and environment HTTP proxies are disabled. Redirects are not
+followed because the payload contains a bearer credential. Connect/read/write
+timeouts are five seconds each and accepted response bodies are limited to
+1 MiB. Expected network failures are logged by bot/room ID and error class,
+without the webhook URL, response body, or credentials. There are no automatic
+webhook retries; handlers should still be idempotent by event/message ID in
+case an operator replays a mention job. Joined deliveries are claimed durably
+before HTTP so a replayed joined job cannot send a second request; a failed or
+interrupted attempt is not retried.
+
+Editing a message does not send another callback. Bot-authored messages do not
+trigger webhooks, preventing bot-to-bot reply loops. These are intentional
+creation-event semantics for now.
+
+## Implementation and credentials
+
+Bots are `User` records with the appended `bot` role, an owning `creator`, and
+a unique, randomly generated 32-character base58 `bot_token`. This reuses the
+existing user/avatar and room membership associations. Bot membership grants
+use normal record creation (not bulk inserts) to run the after-commit join
+callback; human grants retain the bulk path. `User::Bot::RoomJoin` records have a
+unique bot/room key and survive membership revocation to deduplicate joined
+events. They are deleted with the bot or room. Bot users are excluded
+from human account administration and password sign-in. They have an unknown
+random password to satisfy the existing non-null password digest constraint.
+Deleting a bot destroys its user record and associated data, including messages,
+according to the existing user deletion behavior.
+
+Never share bot codes, put them in documentation, or commit them to source
+control. Use secret environment variables in CI and HTTPS in production. Bot
+management responses are `no-store`; the code listing disables Turbo snapshot
+caching. Rails parameter/SQL logging, filtered request paths, and the built-in Sentry
+integration redact codes.
+Because credentials are in the URL, reverse-proxy/access logging and external
+telemetry also need to omit or redact this route's code segment.
+
+Run feature regressions with:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails test test/controllers/bots_controller_test.rb \
+  test/controllers/bots/messages_controller_test.rb \
+  test/lib/rails_ext/bot_credential_filtering_test.rb \
+  test/models/user/bot_test.rb \
+  test/models/user/bot_limit_test.rb \
+  test/models/user/bot/webhook_test.rb \
+  test/models/user/bot/room_membership_test.rb \
+  test/models/user/bot/joined_webhook_test.rb \
+  test/controllers/bots/webhooks_test.rb \
+  test/controllers/autocompletable/users_controller_test.rb
+bin/rails test test/system/managing_bots_test.rb test/system/bot_limits_test.rb
+```
+
+## Automatic bot safeguards
+
+Safeguard limits are fixed in `User::Bot::Safeguards`; there are no account
+settings for them. Apply `bin/rails db:migrate` when deploying this feature.
+
+- A bot may post **10 messages in any 10 seconds**, across all of its rooms.
+  API posts and synchronous webhook replies share the same allowance.
+- The next message is rejected and begins a **30-second cooldown**. API attempts
+  during cooldown return **429 Too Many Requests** with `Retry-After` as a
+  positive integer number of seconds remaining (rounded up). Webhook replies
+  are discarded rather than retried. The rejected message that starts the
+  cooldown is not one of the subsequent attempts.
+- **Five more posting attempts within that same cooldown suspend the bot**.
+  The fifth attempt and subsequent API requests return **403 Forbidden**.
+  Attempts from earlier cooldowns do not contribute. Empty API submissions do
+  not consume the normal posting allowance, but do count as attempts during an
+  existing cooldown.
+- **Ten consecutive failed webhook deliveries suspend the bot**. Any non-`2xx`
+  status (including redirects), timeout, or connection failure counts. Any
+  `2xx` delivery resets the count, even if its body cannot be used as a reply.
+  Mention and joined deliveries share this counter. A reply being rate limited
+  is not a delivery failure.
+
+Suspension uses a separate user status (not deactivation or banning). It leaves
+bot credentials, messages, and memberships intact. Suspended bots remain listed
+with **Suspended** and the reason, and still count toward their owner's bot
+quota. They do not enqueue new webhooks; queued jobs recheck current status
+before HTTP. Requests already in flight cannot be unsent, but their replies are
+checked again before posting. Cooldown expiry does not end suspension.
+
+Only administrators see **Reactivate** on the bots page. It uses Turbo's native
+browser `confirm()` dialog; cancellation does nothing. The server also enforces
+administrator authorization. Reactivating clears the reason, posting history,
+cooldown and both failure/attempt counters without replacing the bot or its
+code. Editing a bot (including fixing its webhook URL) does not reactivate it.
+
+The safeguards are database-backed, not cache-backed, so they are shared across
+API and job processes and survive process restarts. A bot's last ten accepted
+posting timestamps are stored as a bounded JSON list independent of messages,
+so deleting messages cannot bypass the limit. Posting and counters use
+`with_lock`; the configured SQLite immediate transactions serialize writers.
+Posting errors are raised **after** the guard transaction commits to avoid
+rolling back cooldown or suspension state. Never hold the bot lock during HTTP.
+
+Run safeguard regressions with:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails test test/controllers/bots/safeguards_test.rb \
+  test/controllers/bots/reactivations_controller_test.rb \
+  test/models/user/bot/safeguards_concurrency_test.rb
+bin/rails test test/system/bot_safeguards_test.rb
+```
diff --git a/lib/rails_ext/bot_credential_filtering.rb b/lib/rails_ext/bot_credential_filtering.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..f3439e4
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lib/rails_ext/bot_credential_filtering.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,28 @@
+# Parameter filtering doesn't normally redact URL path segments. Bot codes are
+# bearer credentials, so also hide them in Rails request logs and telemetry.
+module BotCredentialFiltering
+  BOT_MESSAGE_PATH = %r{(/rooms/[^/]+/)[^/]+(/messages(?:\?|$))}
+
+  def self.redact(value)
+    value.is_a?(String) ? value.gsub(BOT_MESSAGE_PATH, '\1[FILTERED]\2') : value
+  end
+
+  def self.filter_event(event, _hint)
+    if request = event.request
+      request.url = redact(request.url)
+      request.headers&.transform_values! { |value| redact(value) }
+      request.env&.transform_values! { |value| redact(value) }
+    end
+    event
+  end
+
+  def filtered_path
+    BotCredentialFiltering.redact(super)
+  end
+
+  def filtered_env
+    super.transform_values { |value| BotCredentialFiltering.redact(value) }
+  end
+end
+
+ActionDispatch::Request.prepend(BotCredentialFiltering)
